passages.text:
For eleven years, the clock on the Harmon County Courthouse showed the same time: 4:17. Residents {{1|had long since stopped expecting it to move, they}} gave directions by it ("turn left when the clock says quarter past four") and joked that the county had finally found a way to stop time. {{2|The courthouse itself was built in 1902 from local limestone.}}

Ellison found the mechanism in worse shape than she {{3|expected. Pigeons}} had nested in the gear housing, and decades of grease had hardened into something closer to tar. {{4|Ellison, however, had spent thirty years repairing factory equipment, and she was not discouraged.}} She removed each gear, {{5|soaked it overnight in solvent, and scrubbing}} the teeth with a brass brush.

The hardest problem was a part worn beyond repair: a small toothed wheel, called the escapement wheel, {{6|that release}} the clock's stored energy in measured steps. {{7|Escapement wheels were first used in European clocks centuries ago.}} Ellison {{8|admired}} the worn edges of the broken original, calculated the angle of each tooth, and cut a new wheel by hand over the course of a winter.

On a Saturday morning in June, Ellison set the hands to the correct time and released the pendulum. The clock {{9|began to tick, and it was ticking}}. A small crowd had gathered on the courthouse lawn, and when the minute hand moved for the first time in eleven years, somebody started clapping. {{10|Within a week, a few residents admitted they no longer knew where to turn left.}}
